From 56afc3f67e3b3af219e8833dc3de04481809de8f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Claude (Cowork)" <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 03:37:51 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix: approve/dispute RLS no-op, payment capture, and pro
 payouts
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The "Approve & Release Payment" bug was never a caching problem — the
bookings table has no customer UPDATE RLS policy, so the UPDATE matched
zero rows and PostgREST returned success. Fixed at both ends.

Database (run in Supabase SQL Editor):
- 2026-08-18_customer_booking_update_rls.sql: adds the missing customer
  UPDATE policy plus a guardrail trigger so customers can only approve,
  dispute or cancel — never touch price, payment status or assignment.
- 2026-08-18_payment_plumbing.sql: adds bookings.payment_intent_id,
  payment_captured_at, stripe_transfer_id, provider_paid_at; backfills
  payment_intent_id; repairs the payments.status constraint.
- DIAGNOSE.sql: read-only script to confirm live DB state.

Payments:
- create-payment-intent now writes payment_intent_id onto the booking.
  Nothing ever did, so capture-payment always failed with "No payment
  intent on this booking" and authorized funds expired uncollected.
- Switched pro payouts from destination charges to separate charges and
  transfers. transfer_data needs the pro at PaymentIntent creation, but
  pros are assigned after payment — so every payout went to the platform
  account. capture-payment now creates an idempotent Transfer at capture.
- CheckoutPage treated only 'succeeded' as success; manual capture lands
  on 'requires_capture', so real bookings showed a payment error.
- Fixed invalid enum writes: payment_status 'paid' and payments.status
  'succeeded' both violate CHECK constraints; yard_size_category 'medium'
  is not a valid category.
- stripe-webhook now handles payment_intent.amount_capturable_updated
  (authorization) and computes the platform fee itself.

Customer UI:
- Approve/dispute have separate callbacks. Disputing used to call the
  "mark completed" handler, so a dispute rendered as Completed.
- Approved jobs stay pinned on Track Service instead of vanishing.
- Re-attached RateYourProSection and DisputedWaitingSection, orphaned by
  the CustomerJobCard refactor, plus live tracking and before/after
  photos which were no longer being passed to the status tracker.
- Jobs awaiting review are surfaced on the dashboard and My Services so
  the 24h auto-release window can't run out unseen.

Pro side:
- getProviderEarnings referenced an undefined `profile`, so Pro Earnings
  reported $0 for everyone.
- Removed dead AssignedJobCard.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_0149iXkX2TFSUkgua9goYatV
